WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.663
File names should always be treated as untrusted input.

2
00:00:03.663 --> 00:00:18.783
A user-supplied name may contain path separators, reserved device names, control characters, confusing Unicode, excessive length, misleading extensions, or values that look safe in one operating system and unsafe in another.

3
00:00:18.783 --> 00:00:23.849
The displayed name and the storage name should usually be separate concerns.

4
00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:29.071
A system can preserve an original name as metadata without trusting it as a path.

5
00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:35.150
Path traversal is the risk that input influences file access outside an intended directory.

6
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:45.126
Absolute paths, relative paths, dot-dot segments, encoded separators, symlinks, and platform-specific behavior can all complicate path handling.

7
00:00:45.126 --> 00:00:54.089
Canonicalization concepts can help, but canonicalization must be paired with a containment check against an approved base directory.

8
00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:59.077
The final resolved location should remain where the application intended.

9
00:00:59.077 --> 00:01:03.675
Extension checks and content type claims are useful hints, not proof.

10
00:01:03.675 --> 00:01:06.481
A browser-provided MIME type can be wrong.

11
00:01:06.481 --> 00:01:10.144
A file extension can be misleading or user-controlled.

12
00:01:10.144 --> 00:01:15.288
A document can contain active content even when the extension looks familiar.

13
00:01:15.288 --> 00:01:27.836
Secure validation should use an allowlist of file categories, size limits, content inspection where appropriate, and format-specific checks rather than trusting a single metadata field.

14
00:01:27.836 --> 00:01:30.252
Metadata should be constrained too.

15
00:01:30.252 --> 00:01:42.644
Reject unexpected content types, unsupported extensions, suspicious names, excessive lengths, missing required fields, invalid timestamps, and values that do not match the workflow.

16
00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:49.191
Validation should happen before storage decisions, parsing decisions, logging, or publication.

17
00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:57.453
The earlier the application converts messy file input into controlled metadata, the easier the rest of the workflow becomes.
